.TH DABIPROBE 1 "" "dabiprobe" "User Commands"
.ds AD l
.nr HY 0
.SH NAME
dabiprobe \- check D bindings against C compiler ABI measurements
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B dabiprobe
.RB [ \-\-cc " PATH" ]
.RB [ \-\-dc " PATH" ]
.RB [ \-\-config " NAME" ]
.RB [ \-\-output " DIR" ]
.I MANIFEST.json
.br
.B dabiprobe \-\-help
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B dabiprobe
compiles C and D probe programs and compares the ABI properties selected by a
JSON manifest.
It uses the compilers' own layout and type rules rather than a separate C or D
declaration parser.
.PP
Checks include type size and alignment, selected field offsets and sizes,
field representations, integral values, and function declaration contracts.
Compile-only D reflection can discover members and signatures from the binding.
Optional linked C/D assertions provide separate evidence about chosen behavior.
The C and D compilers must target the same ABI, and their output programs must
be executable on the host.
Linked calls also require compatible object formats, calling conventions, and
C runtime libraries; supply the project's link options through
.BR dFlags .
.PP
A successful run is evidence for the selected checks, compiler configuration,
and test inputs.
It is not certification of an entire binding or API.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BI \-\-cc " PATH"
Select a C compiler executable.
The default is
.B cc
on POSIX hosts and
.B clang
on Windows.
A GCC/Clang-compatible C11 command-line driver is required;
.B cl.exe
and
.B clang-cl
are not accepted driver interfaces.
.TP
.BI \-\-dc " PATH"
Select a D compiler executable.
The default is
.BR ldc2 .
LDC and DMD command-line interfaces are supported.
.TP
.BI \-\-config " NAME"
Run only the named build configuration.
By default, all configurations in the manifest are run.
A manifest without configurations has one configuration named
.BR default .
.TP
.BI \-\-output " DIR"
Save a structured report, configuration snapshots, generated sources, compiler
commands, diagnostics, and a standalone replay script in a new directory.
The parent directory must exist; existing destinations are never overwritten.
Without this option, mismatches, unsupported requests, and probe execution
failures retain private evidence and print its location.
.TP
.B \-\-help
Print usage, options, examples, exit statuses, and safety information, then exit
successfully.
.PP
Compiler options take a single executable name or path, not a shell command.
Quote paths containing spaces when invoking the program from a shell.
Bare names use executable search-path lookup.
Explicit relative compiler paths are anchored to the caller's working directory.
.SH MANIFEST
The manifest must be a JSON object.
Unknown keys, missing required properties, and malformed values are errors.
The
.B headers
and
.B imports
arrays are required, but may be empty.
At least one entry in
.BR types ,
.BR constants ,
.BR functions ,
or
.B calls
is required.
.PP
Include paths, D import paths, and relative paths in compiler flags are resolved
from the manifest's directory, not the caller's working directory.
.SS Build configurations
.B configurations
is an optional nonempty array of objects containing a required
.B name
and optional
.B cFlags
and
.B dFlags
arrays.
Each variant inherits the selected declarations and appends its flags to the
base flags.
Names must be unique and contain no control characters.
.PP
Results, compiler commands, and effective configuration snapshots are kept
separate for each variant.
One failing configuration does not prevent independent variants from running.
The exit status reflects the highest-priority failure across the selected set.
.SS Common properties
.TP
.B headers
Array of C header paths, included with quoted preprocessor include directives.
Quotes, newlines, and NUL characters are not accepted in header paths.
.TP
.B imports
Array of D module names imported by generated D probes.
.TP
.BR cFlags ", " dFlags
Optional arrays of individual compiler arguments.
Arguments are passed literally, without shell splitting, variable expansion,
or wildcard expansion by dabiprobe.
Use these arrays for the include directories, defines, import paths, and ABI
options used by the actual project build.
D link flags and additional D source files may be supplied in
.BR dFlags .
.SS Types and fields
.B types
is an array of objects with required
.B c
and
.B d
type expressions and an optional
.B fields
selection.
Each type pair checks size and alignment, with supported representation checks
where applicable.
.PP
Set
.B fields
to the string
.B "*"
to discover the D aggregate's instance fields without copying their declarations.
Their names select corresponding C members.
Alternatively, provide an array: a field entry is either a string naming the
member on both sides or an object
with
.B c
and
.B d
member names.
Selected fields are checked for offset, size, and supported representation
compatibility, including scalar signedness, array elements, pointers, and
function-pointer contracts.
Unselected D fields are reported as unchecked.
C-only members and padding contents are not automatically enumerated, including
when all D fields are selected.
.PP
Representation predicates use the native C compiler's type-compatibility rules
and layout measurements against the D compiler's reflected types.
Named aggregates use the selected C/D type mappings; this correspondence alone
is not a whole-aggregate or calling-sequence proof.
.PP
For a forward-declared handle, add
.B "\(dqopaque\(dq: true"
to its C/D type mapping and omit
.BR fields .
This establishes nominal correspondence for pointer contracts without requesting
an object size, alignment, or member layout.
The object layout remains explicitly unchecked.
An opaque mapping cannot establish a by-value contract; such requests are
unsupported until a measurable non-opaque type is selected.
.SS Integral values
.B constants
is an array of objects with required
.B c
and
.B d
integral expressions.
Each expression is evaluated once per probe.
Values through 64 bits are compared without conflating negative numbers with
large unsigned values.
Floating-point and wider integral expressions are rejected.
.SS Function contracts
.B functions
is an array of objects selecting C and D declarations.
With only
.B c
and
.B d
names, the D compiler supplies the parameter and return types.
The C compiler independently checks projected signatures using C11 type
compatibility.
Unspecified-parameter declarations, such as
.B "int f()"
in C11, are not treated as a verified parameter count.
No second handwritten function declaration is required for supported inference.
.PP
Explicit contracts remain available.
Provide both
.B returns
and
.BR parameters ,
or neither; supplying only one is an error.
Properties are:
.TP
.BR c ", " d
Required C and D function names or expressions identifying the declarations.
.TP
.B returns
An explicit contract's required object containing
.B c
and
.B d
return types.
Use the literal string
.B void
for a void return; aliases to void are not supported.
.TP
.B parameters
An explicit contract's required array of objects containing
.B c
and
.B d
parameter types.
Use an empty array for a function with no parameters.
Do not use a void parameter entry.
.TP
.B variadic
Optional boolean, defaulting to false.
When true, add a C-style variadic tail after at least one fixed parameter.
Variadic argument promotions are not checked automatically.
.PP
For explicit contracts, the C compiler checks compatibility with the expected
function-pointer type and the D compiler checks conversion to an expected
.B extern(C)
function-pointer type.
Compatible stronger D attributes, such as
.B nothrow
and
.BR @nogc ,
are accepted.
Both declarations must independently satisfy their contracts.
Paired parameter and return sizes, alignments, and scalar kinds must also agree.
These checks do not call the functions.
.PP
Inference has explicit bounds: unsupported qualifiers or callable forms, missing
aggregate mappings, excessive type nesting, and signatures requiring more than
256 complete candidates are reported as unsupported rather than guessed.
Variadic and ref/out-style signatures require explicit contracts where supported.
Native C layout predicates guard inferred integer alternatives.
.PP
Complex C declarators require named typedefs.
D static-array parameters are rejected because C array parameters decay to
pointers while D static arrays are passed differently.
Use an explicit pointer contract where appropriate.
.SS Executable interop checks
.TP
.B cSource
Optional C source text compiled into an object and linked into D probes.
The manifest's headers are included first.
An include directive such as
.B "#include \(dqapi.c\(dq"
allows implementation code to remain in an ordinary source file.
.TP
.B dSource
Optional module-level D declarations or helpers appended after the imports.
.TP
.B calls
Optional array of objects with a descriptive
.B name
and a D expression in
.BR d .
The expression's type must be
.BR bool ,
and it must evaluate to true.
.PP
Explicit calls are compiled and run in a separate program only after passive
layout, type, constant, and declaration checks succeed without unsupported
requested contracts.
A disagreement or unsupported request skips the explicit assertion stage.
The tool runs the supplied expressions; it does not infer which functions or
inputs they cover or invent calls to arbitrary library functions.
.SH OUTPUT
Results are written to standard output.
Each check identifies its configuration, declaration, property, and outcome.
Measured properties and executed behavior assertions have separate categories
and summary counts.
.TP
.B checked
A selected measurement agrees, or an explicit boolean assertion returned true.
.TP
.B mismatched
A measurement or assertion disagrees.
.TP
.B unchecked
A property was not examined, or execution was deliberately skipped.
.TP
.B unsupported
A requested contract cannot be represented by the checker.
.TP
.B failed_execution
An input or execution operation prevented verification.
.PP
Unchecked and unsupported records are not counted as successful checks.
Partial coverage is labeled explicitly.
For inferred representation predicates, 1 means compatibility holds and 0 means
it does not.
These predicates may also emit 2 for an unsupported unprototyped callable;
that record is not counted as agreement or as an ordinary value mismatch.
The report also records the reflected D type, C expression, and checking policy.
These are compiler-derived checks, not observations of library behavior.
.PP
Compiler diagnostics and fatal errors are written to standard error.
Generated probe output is indexed and validated.
Unexpected output, including output from constructors or helpers, is an error
rather than an additional measurement.
.SH EVIDENCE AND REPLAY
An evidence bundle contains
.BR report.json ,
.BR manifest.json ,
.BR commands.jsonl ,
per-configuration sources and command logs, and
.BR replay.py .
The versioned JSON report retains effective flags, working directories, compiler
identification commands and logs, per-check outcomes, and source hashes.
.PP
Run
.B "python3 replay.py"
to rebuild and execute the captured checks without dabiprobe.
The script uses fresh private scratch and never executes retained binaries.
It supports
.BR \-\-config ,
.BR \-\-workdir ,
.BR \-\-cc ,
and
.B \-\-dc
overrides for selecting or relocating an equivalent setup.
Windows replay requires Python 3.13 or newer for private-directory support.
.PP
Headers, D imports, libraries, response files, compiler installations and
configuration, and other dependencies remain external.
Replay is not a source-dependency archive or a new probe generator.
It compares fresh D discovery metadata with the captured metadata and refuses
stale representation predicates when declarations or target facts change.
Run dabiprobe again to generate checks for the new configuration.
.PP
Only a named allowlist of build/search/runtime environment variables is saved.
Replay inherits its current environment unless
.B \-\-recorded-env
is explicitly selected.
Paths, flags, source fragments and diagnostics may contain sensitive project
information; review bundles before sharing them.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
No disagreement was found in supported executed checks, or help was displayed.
Inspect coverage: this does not mean unexamined properties were verified.
.TP
.B 1
At least one ABI measurement or explicit call assertion failed.
.TP
.B 2
Invalid input, compiler or linker failure, probe execution error, malformed
probe output, evidence-export failure, or another operational error prevented
verification.
.TP
.B 3
A requested check was unsupported or remained incomplete.
Unselected coverage notes alone do not produce this status.
.PP
For multiple configurations, execution/input errors take priority over mismatches,
which take priority over unsupported or otherwise incomplete requests.
.SH EXAMPLES
Run the supplied layout and integral-value example:
.PP
.EX
dabiprobe examples/packet/manifest.json
.EE
.PP
Use Clang and LDC for the aggregate and callback example:
.PP
.EX
dabiprobe \-\-cc clang \-\-dc ldc2 examples/interop/manifest.json
.EE
.PP
Select an explicitly installed D compiler:
.PP
.EX
dabiprobe \-\-dc /opt/ldc/bin/ldc2 path/to/manifest.json
.EE
.PP
Capture one project configuration and replay its checks:
.PP
.EX
dabiprobe \-\-config release \-\-output results binding-checks.json
python3 results/replay.py
.EE
.PP
A minimal manifest checking a scalar type:
.PP
.EX
{
  "headers": ["stdint.h"],
  "imports": [],
  "types": [{"c": "int32_t", "d": "int"}]
}
.EE
.SH SECURITY
Run only trusted manifests, headers, D modules, source fragments, and compiler
flags.
Compilation and probe execution can execute code with the caller's privileges.
The program is not a sandbox and does not impose a subprocess time limit.
.PP
Imported modules and linked code may execute constructors before passive results
are available.
The failure gate protects only the explicit
.B calls
stage; it does not prevent arbitrary code in trusted declarations or constructors
from running.
.PP
Probe files are written to private temporary directories.
Successful runs remove scratch; explicit evidence export also permits scratch
cleanup after a mismatch or error.
Without export, unsuccessful probe runs retain their private workspace and
evidence for diagnosis.
The printed path can be removed after the evidence is no longer needed.
Forced termination may also leave temporary files behind.
On Windows, temporary storage must support a protected current-user-only ACL.
Filesystems that cannot enforce these permissions are rejected before probe
files are written.
.SH LIMITATIONS
Checks describe the selected compiler target and build configuration, not a
running kernel, another build, or every supported target.
Matching pointer size and endianness alone does not establish ABI compatibility.
Use the include paths, defines, packing, and target options from the actual build.
.PP
Unselected fields, C-member completeness, padding contents, bitfields, flexible
array members, ownership, lifetimes, other calling conventions, and cross-target
execution are not automatically verified.
Unsupported representations are explicit in the report.
Character encoding and signed-character value semantics are not inferred from
pairing D char with C char.
Floating-point checks do not prove all encodings or exceptional-value behavior.
Explicit assertions provide evidence for their selected inputs, not all possible
inputs or every property of a prebuilt library.
